aches\n- The manifest type supports `MORPH_RELATION` fields with an explicit `morphId` on any object (including standard objects): `packages/twenty-shared/src/application/fieldManifestType.ts:48-64`.\n- The SDK exposes `FieldType.FILES` and validates FILES fields: `packages/twenty-sdk/src/sdk/define/fields/validate-fields.ts:42-43`, `.../validate-fields.ts:214-228`.\n- Front components can upload directly to a FILES field: `packages/twenty-sdk/src/sdk/front-component/globals/frontComponentHostCommunicationApi.ts:123-147` (`UploadFileParams.fieldMetadataId`, comment lines 131-134: uploads must target a FILES field or strand a temporary orphan).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Concrete takeaways for a chat message attachment feature\n\n1. Reuse the single `attachment` morph id `20202020-f634-435d-ab8d-e1168b375c69`; do not create parallel storage.\n2. `chatMessage.attachments` and `attachment.targetChatMessage` are expected to already exist automatically after install (object-create side-effect handler; see section 2 and the app-sync integration test). Verify at install time, since the TypeScript `AttachmentWorkspaceEntity` does not enumerate it.\n3. The upload path to reuse is FILES-field direct upload: `createFileUpload`/`completeFileUpload` with `fileFolder: FileFolder.FilesField` and a `fieldMetadataId` (or `FileFolder.AgentChat` for AI-chat style uploads), then create an `attachment` row pointing at the target via `targetChatMessageId` with `file: [{ fileId, label }]` — mirroring `useUploadAttachmentFile.tsx:28-67`.\n4. Source attribution today is `FileEntity.applicationId` + the storage path namespace and the morph target; an explicit `sourceApp` attachment field does not exist (planned P6.1, PLAN.md:988).\n5. Reuse existing tests as templates: `attachments.integration-spec.ts`, `files-field-sync.integration-spec.ts`, `files-field-download.integration-spec.ts`, `direct-file-upload.integration-spec.ts`, and the app manifest integrity test pattern.\n\nNo code was written or modified during this investigation.\n</task_result>\n</task>"
