        "result": "<task id=\"ses_f49fb8e93ffe1fZI6d6CEzbhHj\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nI have completed a thorough investigation. Here is the full report.\n\n---\n\n# GraphQL integration tests in `twenty-server` — investigation report\n\n## 0. Executive summary / things that will bite you\n\n1. **There is no chat integration spec yet.** `P5.1` explicitly lists \"Integration tests: pagination, isolation, unread counts\" as an open bullet (`PLAN.md:958`), and `docs/plan/phases/phase-05-report.md:14` calls the live introspection + integration tests \"the separate `P5.1-chat-attachments-tests` story\" (also listed in `.ralph-tui/session.json:64`).\n2. **The chat objects are app-owned, not engine-owned.** `chatMessage`/`chatChannel`/etc. only exist in a workspace after the `a2e-chat` application is installed. No dev-seeder installs `a2e-chat` (`grep` for `chatMessage|chatChannel|a2e-chat` in `src/engine/workspace-manager` returns nothing). Your spec must install the app (or sync a synthetic equivalent) before `chatMessages` can resolve.\n3. **Likely schema-scope/collision blocker on the custom resolver.** `ChatResolver` is `@MetadataResolver()` (scope `metadata`), but there is already an AI `chatMessages(threadId)` query in the same metadata schema. The Nest GraphQL `groupMetadata` step silently last-wins on duplicate root fields. See §5.4 — verify by introspection before writing tests.\n4. **\"Unread counts\" are not exposed by any GraphQL query.** `aggregateUnreadCounts` is only consumed by `ChatRealtimePublisherService.publishReadEvent` (realtime read event). A GraphQL integration test can't assert an unread count directly today; only the pure unit spec covers it.\n5. `deleteAllRecords` hardcodes the seeded Apple workspace schema, so it won't help on a freshly created second workspace.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Integration test infrastructure\n\n### 1.1 Directory layout\n\nAll GraphQL integration specs live under:\n\n```\npackages/twenty-server/test/integration/graphql/\n├── codegen/                      # introspection-based spec scaffolder\n│   ├── index.ts\n│   ├── introspection-query.ts\n│   └── introspection.interface.ts\n├── suites/                       # the specs (testRegex *.integration-spec.ts)\n└── utils/                        # 84 request/operation/assertion helpers\n```\n\n`suites/` contains ~50 entries, including `object-generated/` (metadata-engine CRUD specs such as `rockets.integration-spec.ts`, `objects.integration-spec.ts`), `search/`, `workspace/`, `auth/`, `view/`, `workflow/`, `hooks/`, `upsert/`, `onboarding/`, `document-share/`, `settings-permissions/`, `event-logs/`, plus top-level cross-cutting specs (`cursor-pagination-order-by.integration-spec.ts`, `filter-by-relation-field.integration-spec.ts`, `order-by-with-group-by.integration-spec.ts`, `timeline-from-object-record.integration-spec.ts`, etc.).\n\nKey point: **there is no `chat/` directory and no `chat*.integration-spec.ts` anywhere** (verified by glob and content search).\n\n### 1.2 Representative spec: cursor pagination\n\n`test/integration/graphql/suites/cursor-pagination-order-by.integration-spec.ts` (592 lines) is the best template for pagination. The pagination driver is `paginateForward` (lines 54–96):\n\n```ts\nconst paginateForward = async ({ orderBy, objectMetadataSingularName = 'opportunity', ... }) => {\n  const ids: string[] = [];\n  let after: string | undefined = undefined;\n  for (let iteration = 0; iteration < 20; iteration++) {\n    const response = await makeGraphqlAPIRequest(\n      findManyOperationFactory({ ..., orderBy, first, after }),\n    ).expect(200);\n    const connection = response.body.data[objectMetadataPluralName];\n    ids.push(...connection.edges.map((edge) => edge.node.id));\n    if (!connection.pageInfo.hasNextPage) break;\n    after = connection.pageInfo.endCursor;\n  }\n  return { ids, pages };\n};\n```\n\nAssertions cover: every record returned, `hasNextPage`/`endCursor` chaining, cursor from a non-selected orderBy field (lines 247–267), NULL blocks, duplicate sort values, composite FK/sub-field ordering. This is the pattern to mirror for `chatMessages`'s `{ channelId, limit, after }` keyset.\n\n### 1.3 Representative spec: custom resolver + permissions/isolation\n\n`test/integration/graphql/suites/search/search-resolver.integration-spec.ts` (1485 lines) exercises a custom resolver end-to-end, including cursor encode/decode by importing the production cursor utils (`src/engine/api/graphql/graphql-query-runner/utils/cursors.util`, lines 35–41) and calling the formatter through `test/integration/graphql/utils/search.util.ts` / `search-factory.util.ts`.\n\n`test/integration/metadata/suites/calendar-channel/calendar-channel-resolver.integration-spec.ts` (100 lines) is a compact example of asserting only-the-caller's records and FORBIDDEN on a foreign user (lines 9–50, 79–98). This is a good structural model for \"cross-workspace isolation\" assertions.\n\nThe **closest analog to `chatMessages`** (a custom record-level connection plus a cross-cutting concern) is `search-resolver` (custom connection + cursor) combined with `calendar-channel-resolver` (caller scoping).\n\n### 1.4 Codegen helper for new object-generated specs\n\n`test/integration/graphql/codegen/index.ts` introspects `http://localhost:3000/graphql` and writes generated CRUD specs into `test/integration/graphql/suites/object-generated`. Run via `npx nx run twenty-server:generate:integration-test` (target at `packages/twenty-server/project.json:249-254`). This is for metadata-generated CRUD, not custom resolvers, so it will not scaffold `chatMessages`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Configuration, authentication, and the standard spec pattern\n\n### 2.1 Jest config\n\n`packages/twenty-server/jest-integration.config.ts`:\n- `testRegex: '\\\\.integration-spec\\\\.ts$'` (line 36)\n- `globalSetup: '<rootDir>/test/integration/utils/setup-test.ts'` (line 38)\n- `globalTeardown: '<rootDir>/test/integration/utils/teardown-test.ts'` (line 39)\n- `setupFilesAfterEach: '<rootDir>/test/integration/utils/setup-wait-for-all-jobs-between-tests.ts'` (line 40)\n- `testTimeout: 20000`, `maxWorkers: 1`, `maxConcurrency: 1` (lines 25, 41–42)\n- `dotenv.config({ path: '.env.test' })` when `NODE_ENV=test` (lines 8–12)\n- globals `APP_PORT: 4000`, `NODE_ENV: TEST`, and all tokens from `test/integration/constants/test-tokens.json` (lines 88–94)\n\n`setup-test.ts` boots the **real `AppModule`** with `createApp({})`, calls `rawDataSource.initialize()`, listens on `APP_PORT`, and exposes `global.app` and `global.testDataSource`:\n\n```ts\nconst app = await createApp({});\nawait rawDataSource.initialize();\nawait app.listen(projectConfig.globals.APP_PORT as number);\nglobal.app = app;\nglobal.testDataSource = rawDataSource;\n```\n\n`setup-wait-for-all-jobs-between-tests.ts` runs `waitForAllJobsToFinish()` in `beforeAll` and `afterEach`, and closes queue connections in `afterAll` (150s timeout).\n\n`create-app.ts` (test/integration/utils/create-app.ts) overrides `StripeSDKService`, `ExceptionHandlerService`, `CaptchaDriverFactory` and applies real CORS/body-parser settings.\n\n### 2.2 Request helpers (the \"client\")\n\nThere is no `client`/`graphqlRequest`/`signIn` helper by those names. The equivalents:\n\n- `test/integration/graphql/utils/make-graphql-api-request.util.ts` — POSTs to `/graphql`; defaults to `APPLE_JANE_ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN` (line 12); optional 2nd arg is a token. This is for **workspace record CRUD and core resolvers**.\n- `test/integration/metadata/suites/utils/make-metadata-api-request.util.ts` — POSTs to `/metadata`; default token `APPLE_JANE_ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN`; pass explicit token for callers. This is for **metadata/app/resolver-on-metadata**.\n\nTokens (`test/integration/constants/test-tokens.json`): `APPLE_JANE_ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `APPLE_JONY_MEMBER_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `APPLE_PHIL_GUEST_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `API_KEY_ACCESS_TOKEN`, expired/invalid variants. These are seeded, per-workspace JWTs for the Apple workspace.\n\n### 2.3 Workspace context / sign-in helpers\n\n- `test/integration/graphql/utils/sign-up-in-workspace-and-get-access-token.util.ts` — signs a user into the **Apple** workspace (invite hash `apple.dev-invite-hash`, `SEED_APPLE_WORKSPACE_ID`), marks email verified via `testDataSource.query`, then exchanges login token for an access token (lines 12–99).\n- `test/integration/graphql/utils/sign-up-in-new-workspace.util.ts` — `signUpInNewWorkspace({ accessToken, displayName })` calls the metadata mutation and returns `loginToken` + workspace (lines 15–63).\n- `test/integration/utils/activate-workspace.util.ts`, `get-auth-tokens-from-login-token.util.ts`, `sign-up.util.ts`, `delete-user.util.ts` round out the lifecycle.\n- Seeded workspace IDs come from `src/engine/workspace-manager/dev-seeder/core/constants/seeder-workspaces.constant` (`SEED_APPLE_WORKSPACE_ID`, `SEED_YCOMBINATOR_WORKSPACE_ID`).\n\n### 2.4 Standard pattern a new spec uses\n\n```ts\nimport { makeGraphqlAPIRequest } from 'test/integration/graphql/utils/make-graphql-api-request.util';\nimport { createManyOperationFactory } from 'test/integration/graphql/utils/create-many-operation-factory.util';\nimport { findManyOperationFactory } from 'test/integration/graphql/utils/find-many-operation-factory.util';\n\ndescribe('...', () => {\n  beforeAll(async () => { /* cleanup + seed records via GraphQL */ });\n  afterAll(async () => { /* best-effort cleanup */ });\n\n  it('...', async () => {\n    const response = await makeGraphqlAPIRequest(/* operation factory */).expect(200);\n    expect(response.body.errors).toBeUndefined();\n    expect(response.body.data...)....\n  });\n});\n```\n\nOperation factories available (all in `test/integration/graphql/utils/`): `create-one|create-many|update-one|update-many|delete-one|delete-many|destroy-one|destroy-many|restore-one|restore-many|find-one|find-many|group-by|merge-many|search(-factory)` plus `-operation-factory.util` and `-operation.util` variants. Exact signatures (selected):\n- `createManyOperationFactory({ objectMetadataSingularName, objectMetadataPluralName, gqlFields, data, upsert })` → mutation `create<Plural>(data, upsert)` (lines 4–29).\n- `createOneOperationFactory({ objectMetadataSingularName, gqlFields, data })` → `create<Singular>(data)` (lines 4–24).\n- `findManyOperationFactory({ objectMetadataSingularName, objectMetadataPluralName, gqlFields, filter, orderBy, after, before, first, last })` (lines 4–52).\n\nAssertion helpers: `graphql-test-assertions.util.ts` (`assertGraphQLSuccessfulResponse`, `assertGraphQLErrorResponse`, `assertGraphQLErrorResponseWithSnapshot`). Other useful utils: `test/integration/utils/find-records-by-filter.util.ts` (`findRecordNodesByFilter`, `findRecordIdsByFilter`), `delete-all-records.ts` (note hardcoded schema — §6.4), `delete-records-by-ids.ts`, `expect-eventually.util.ts`, `get-core-repository.util.ts`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Running a single integration spec\n\n### 3.1 Nx targets (`packages/twenty-server/project.json`)\n\n```jsonc\n\"test:integration\": {\n  \"executor\": \"nx:run-commands\",\n  \"options\": {\n    \"cwd\": \"packages/twenty-server\",\n    \"commands\": [\"NODE_ENV=test NODE_OPTIONS=\\\"--max-old-space-size=6144\\\" nx jest --config ./jest-integration.config.ts --logHeapUsage\"]\n  },\n  \"configurations\": {\n    \"with-db-reset\": {\n      \"cwd\": \"packages/twenty-server\",\n      \"commands\": [\n        \"NODE_ENV=test NODE_OPTIONS=\\\"--max-old-space-size=12288 --import tsx/esm\\\" nx database:reset > reset-logs.log\",\n        \"NODE_ENV=test NODE_OPTIONS=\\\"--max-old-space-size=6144\\\" nx jest --config ./jest-integration.config.ts --logHeapUsage\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`test:integration:secure` uses `jest-integration-secure.config.ts`. `jest` itself is an `nx:run-commands` target with `cwd: packages/twenty-server`, `command: \"jest\"` (`project.json:213-219`).\n\n`database:reset` (`project.json`, target) depends on `build`, truncates + reinitializes + flushes cache, and (default `seed` config) runs `workspace:seed:dev`, which creates the seeded Apple workspace (schema `workspace_1wgvd1injqtife6y4rvfbu3h5`).\n\n### 3.2 Exact commands\n\nFull suite with reset (recommended for a chat spec, because it guarantees the seeded workspace and a clean DB):\n\n```bash\nnpx nx run twenty-server:test:integration:with-db-reset\n```\n\nSingle spec, no reset (after the DB has been reset/started once):\n\n```bash\n# from packages/twenty-server (dotenv in jest-integration.config.ts and setup-test.ts\n# resolve '.env.test' relative to cwd, so cwd must be packages/twenty-server):\nNODE_ENV=test npx jest --config ./jest-integration.config.ts \\\n  test/integration/graphql/suites/chat/chat-messages.integration-spec.ts --runInBand\n```\n\nOr via Nx with a path filter:\n\n```bash\nnpx nx run twenty-server:test:integration --testPathPattern=\"chat-messages\"\n```\n\nReset then a single spec:\n\n```bash\nnpx nx run twenty-server:database:reset\nNODE_ENV=test npx jest --config ./jest-integration.config.ts <spec> --runInBand   # cwd packages/twenty-server\n```\n\n### 3.3 Environment requirements\n\n- **`NODE_ENV=test` is required** — otherwise the config loads `.env` instead of `.env.test`.\n- **Postgres + Redis are required**, and the DB should be reset/seeded for integration runs. `packages/twenty-server/.env.test` sets `PG_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/test`, `REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379`, and `REDIS_QUEUE_URL=.../1` (queue isolation from dev workers). Setup script: `bash packages/twenty-utils/setup-dev-env.sh`.\n- Realtime-specific specs use a separate config (`jest-integration-realtime.config.ts`, `testRegex: 'realtime-.*integration-spec\\\\.ts$'`, no globalSetup/DB) — note its header comment says the shared harness \"boots the full AppModule in globalSetup and currently fails on this branch with a pre-existing TDZ in the workspace-query-runner import graph\". Worth verifying the normal harness actually boots before investing in a spec. \n- `twenty-shared/dist` is per-branch state: run `npx nx build twenty-shared --skip-nx-cache` if a dependent typecheck/test looks stale (AGENTS.md gotcha).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cross-workspace isolation — existing examples\n\nThere is no test named \"cross-workspace\"; the canonical example is `test/integration/graphql/suites/search/search-caller-permissions.integration-spec.ts` (225 lines). It mints a **foreign-workspace token** by forging a JWT for a user who holds roles in both the Apple and YCombinator seeded workspaces (`buildSeededUserAccessToken`, lines 34–62), then asserts no leakage:\n\n```ts\nconst foreignWorkspaceAdminToken = buildSeededUserAccessToken({\n  workspaceId: YCOMBINATOR_WORKSPACE_ID,\n  userWorkspaceId: TIM_YCOMBINATOR_USER_WORKSPACE_ID,\n});\n...\nit('never lets a foreign-workspace member see Apple titles', async () => {\n  const response = await searchFor({ searchInput: appleCompanyName, objectNameSingular: 'company', token: foreignWorkspaceAdminToken });\n  expect(JSON.stringify(response.body)).not.toContain(appleCompanyName);\n});\n```\n\nIt also creates a record *in the foreign workspace* (`foreignCompanyResponse`, lines 146–151) and proves it's visible to that workspace while Apple titles are not (lines 205–224). The token forge helper is `test/integration/graphql/utils/forge-legacy-hs256-token.util.ts`.\n\nSecond example: `test/integration/metadata/suites/application/enqueue-job.integration-spec.ts` (720 lines) actually **creates a second workspace** (`createOtherWorkspaceApplicationToken`, lines 125–182: `signUp` → mark verified → `signUpInNewWorkspace` → `getAuthTokensFromLoginToken` → `activateWorkspace`) and asserts the foreign workspace's `getJobs` is empty (lines ~652–661).\n\nThird example: `test/integration/metadata/suites/application/dev-mode-foreign-registration.integration-spec.ts` seeds a registration owned by `SEED_YCOMBINATOR_WORKSPACE_ID` (line 12) and asserts the Apple admin can't touch it.\n\nFor chat, the service-level isolation contract is in `ChatMessageService.listChannelMessages`: it checks `context.authContext.workspace.id !== workspaceId` and throws `ForbiddenException` (`chat-message.service.ts:72-76`), and the WHERE clause deliberately omits `workspaceId` because tables are per-workspace schemas (lines 91–106). An integration test should prove a foreign token cannot read Apple `chatMessage` rows / gets no data.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The custom chat resolver, DTOs, and service\n\n### 5.1 Resolver\n\n`packages/twenty-server/src/modules/chat/resolvers/chat.resolver.ts` (73 lines):\n\n```ts\n@MetadataResolver()\n@UsePipes(ResolverValidationPipe)\n@UseFilters(AuthGraphqlApiExceptionFilter)\n@UseGuards(WorkspaceAuthGuard, CustomPermissionGuard)\nexport class ChatResolver {\n  constructor(private readonly chatMessageService: ChatMessageService,\n              private readonly chatTypingService: ChatTypingService) {}\n\n  @Query(() => ChatMessageConnectionDTO)\n  async chatMessages(\n    @AuthWorkspace() workspace: WorkspaceEntity,\n    @Args() { channelId, limit, after }: ChatMessagesArgs,\n  ): Promise<ChatMessageConnectionDTO> {\n    return this.chatMessageService.listChannelMessages({ workspaceId: workspace.id, channelId, limit, after });\n  }\n\n  @Mutation(() => ChatTypingIndicatorDTO)\n  async sendChatTypingIndicator(\n    @AuthWorkspace() workspace: WorkspaceEntity,\n    @AuthWorkspaceMemberId() workspaceMemberId: string | undefined,\n    @Args('input') input: SendChatTypingInput,\n  ): Promise<ChatTypingIndicatorDTO> { ... }\n}\n```\n\nOnly these two operations exist. No unread-count query.\n\n### 5.2 Args\n\n`src/modules/chat/dtos/chat-messages.args.ts`:\n- `channelId: string` — required, `@Field(() => UUIDScalarType)`, `@IsUUID()`\n- `limit?: number` — optional Int, min 1, max `MAX_CHAT_MESSAGE_PAGE_SIZE` (100)\n- `after?: string` — optional opaque cursor\n\n`limit` and `after` are the keyset pagination args (the service calls it a \"Keyset args, mirroring the metadata findMany connection\").\n\n### 5.3 Service and cursor\n\n`src/modules/chat/services/chat-message.service.ts`:\n- `DEFAULT_CHAT_MESSAGE_PAGE_SIZE = 50`, `MAX_CHAT_MESSAGE_PAGE_SIZE = 100` (lines 19–20)\n- `listChannelMessages({ workspaceId, channelId, limit?, after? }): Promise<ChatMessagePage>` (lines 52–128)\n- Selects exactly: `id, body, channelId, authorId, threadParentId, createdAt, editedAt, deletedAt` (lines 114–123)\n- Order `{ createdAt: 'DESC', id: 'DESC' }`, fetches `pageSize + 1` (lines 112–113)\n- Resume predicate uses `(createdAt < cursor.createdAt) OR (createdAt = cursor.createdAt AND id < cursor.id)` (lines 94–106)\n\n`src/modules/chat/utils/chat-message-cursor.util.ts`: `encodeChatMessageCursor`, `decodeChatMessageCursor` (base64url JSON `{createdAt,id}`, returns null on malformed input), `buildChatMessagePage({ records, limit })` (lines 50–71). This is importable from a spec to build/verify cursors (same as the search spec imports `encodeCursorData`).\n\nDTO types: `ChatMessageConnectionDTO` (`edges: ChatMessageEdgeDTO[]`, `pageInfo: ChatMessagePageInfoDTO`), `ChatMessageEdgeDTO` (`node: ChatMessageDTO`, `cursor: String`), `ChatMessagePageInfoDTO` (`hasNextPage: Boolean`, `endCursor: String` nullable). Internal types in `types/chat-message.type.ts`.\n\n### 5.4 ⚠️ Schema-scope / field-name collision (must verify before writing tests)\n\n- `ChatResolver` is decorated `@MetadataResolver()` → sets `RESOLVER_SCHEMA_SCOPE = 'metadata'` (`metadata-resolver.decorator.ts:7-11`).\n- The core `/graphql` schema only includes scope `'core'` (`graphql-config.service.ts:85-86`), so `ChatResolver` is **excluded from `/graphql`**.\n- The metadata `/metadata` schema includes scope `'metadata'` (`metadata.module-factory.ts:39`). Its `include: [MetadataGraphQLApiModule]` transitively reaches `CoreEngineModule` via `GraphQLConfigModule` (`graphql-config.module.ts:7`), which imports `ChatModule`, so `ChatResolver` **is** included in `/metadata`.\n- But `AgentChatResolver` is **also** scope `metadata` and defines `chatMessages(threadId)` returning `[AgentMessageDTO]` (`agent-chat.resolver.ts:58, 96-107`). Both are on the metadata `Query` type.\n- The patched `@nestjs/graphql` `BaseExplorerService.groupMetadata` (node_modules/@nestjs/graphql/dist/services/base-explorer.service.js) folds fields by name with last-wins, so one `chatMessages` **silently shadows** the other depending on module/provider order — no startup error.\n\nThe P5.1 phase report itself lists \"live `dev:build`+install and real-session GraphQL introspection of ... `chatMessages`\" as unverified Tier-2 work (`phase-05-report.md:14`). Recommendation: before writing the spec, boot the app and introspect both endpoints / check the resolver ordering to determine whether `chatMessages(channelId, limit, after)` is actually reachable on `/metadata`. If it is shadowed, the custom resolver needs to move to `@CoreResolver()` (and `/graphql`), or be renamed; that is a production change, outside the spec. The frontend `packages/twenty-front/src/generated-metadata/graphql.ts:4835` currently binds `chatMessages` to the AI `AgentMessage` shape, which is consistent with the AI resolver winning.\n\n### 5.5 Unread counts\n\n`src/modules/chat/utils/chat-unread-count.util.ts` — pure `aggregateUnreadCounts({ messages, readPositions, workspaceMemberId })` (lines 24–67); ignores soft-deleted, own messages, and messages at/before `lastReadAt`, keyed by channel. It is consumed only by `ChatRealtimePublisherService.resolveUnreadCount` (`chat-realtime-publisher.service.ts:223-270`) and embedded in the realtime read event `read.unreadCount` (`publishReadEvent`, lines 156–193). Unit coverage exists (`utils/__tests__/chat-unread-count.util.spec.ts`). **There is no GraphQL field returning unread counts**, so \"unread counts\" cannot be integration-tested through the chat resolver as-is; it would need the realtime read-event path (real `/realtime` subscription, see `jest-integration-realtime.config.ts`) or a new resolver.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The `a2e-chat` app objects and how to create them in a test\n\n### 6.1 Object definitions\n\n`packages/twenty-apps/internal/a2e-chat/src/objects/`:\n- `chat-message.object.ts` — `nameSingular: 'chatMessage'`, `namePlural: 'chatMessages'`, fields `body` (TEXT, label identifier), `editedAt` (DATE_TIME nullable), `channel` (MANY_TO_ONE, `joinColumnName: 'channelId'`, CASCADE), `author` (MANY_TO_ONE to workspaceMember, `joinColumnName: 'authorId'`, SET_NULL), `threadParent`/`threadReplies` self-relation (`joinColumnName: 'threadParentId'`), `reactions`, `readCursors`.\n- `chat-channel.object.ts` — `chatChannel`, fields `name`, `kind`, `visibility`, `topic`, `postingRoles`, relations `messages`/`members`/`readCursors`.\n- `chat-channel-member.object.ts` — `chatChannelMember` junction (`membershipChannelId`, `membershipWorkspaceMemberId`).\n- `chat-read-cursor.object.ts` — `chatReadCursor`, fields `lastReadAt`, `channel` (`readCursorChannelId`), `workspaceMember` (`readCursorWorkspaceMemberId`), `lastReadMessage` (`lastReadMessageId`).\n- `chat-reaction.object.ts`, workspaceMember inverse fields in `src/fields/`.\n\nThese are declared with the SDK (`defineObject`, `FieldType`, `OnDeleteAction`) and are **metadata-engine objects**, i.e. installing the app provisions workspace tables and generates typed CRUD resolvers. The join columns (`channelId`, `authorId`, `threadParentId`, `readCursorChannelId`) are exactly what `ChatMessageService` selects.\n\n### 6.2 Build output / manifest\n\n`packages/twenty-apps/internal/a2e-chat/.twenty/output/` contains a real built `manifest.json` (5 objects: `chatChannel`, `chatChannelMember`, `chatMessage`, `chatReaction`, `chatReadCursor`; application universal identifier `e2dce399-87f1-4548-b307-5f368b4d5dd4` from `src/application.config.ts`). The generated `chatMessage` field entries confirm `universalSettings.joinColumnName` of `channelId`/`authorId`/`threadParentId`.\n\n### 6.3 Two ways a test can create the objects\n\n**Option A — install the real app (mirrors `bilan-manifest-sync.integration-spec.ts`).** That spec reads the real built manifest from `packages/twenty-apps/internal/a2e-accounting/.twenty/output/manifest.json`, calls `setupApplicationForSync(...)` in `beforeEach`, uploads built resources, calls `syncApplicationQueryFactory({ manifest })` via `makeMetadataAPIRequest`, and cleans up with `cleanupApplicationAndAppRegistration(...)` (lines 6–9, 55–90, 62–82). It also run logs a `digest` of sync errors. Reuse this exact structure with `a2e-chat`'s `.twenty/output/manifest.json` and APPLICATION_UNIVERSAL_IDENTIFIER.\n\n**Option B — synthetic manifest (mirrors `test/integration/graphql/suites/upsert/composite-unique-index-upsert.integration-spec.ts`).** Build a minimal `chatMessage` (and `chatChannel`/`chatReadCursor` as needed) with `buildDefaultObjectManifest({ applicationUniversalIdentifier, nameSingular, namePlural, labelSingular, labelPlural, icon, additionalFields })`, wrap it in `buildBaseManifest({ appId, roleId, overrides: { objects: [...] } })`, then:\n```ts\nawait setupApplicationForSync({ applicationUniversalIdentifier, name, description, sourcePath });\nawait syncApplication({ manifest, expectToFail: false });\n...\nawait cleanupApplicationAndAppRegistration({ applicationUniversalIdentifier });\n```\n(beforeAll/afterAll at spec lines 227–243). `additionalFields` entries need `universalIdentifier`, `type: FieldMetadataType.TEXT` etc.; relations use the SDK manifest settings shape (`relationType: 'MANY_TO_ONE'`, `joinColumnName: 'channelId'`, target universal identifiers). `field-vocabulary.ts` in a2e-chat shows the `manyToOne(joinColumnName)` builder.\n\nOnce the object exists, create records through the generated mutation on `/graphql` with `makeGraphqlAPIRequest(createManyOperationFactory({ objectMetadataSingularName: 'chatMessage', objectMetadataPluralName: 'chatMessages', gqlFields, data }))`. Then call the custom resolver query.\n\n### 6.4 Important gotchas for seeding\n\n- `test/integration/utils/delete-all-records.ts` hardcodes `TEST_SCHEMA_NAME = 'workspace_1wgvd1injqtife6y4rvfbu3h5'` (the seeded Apple workspace). It only works if you installed the app into that workspace. For a second (isolation) workspace you must clean up through GraphQL destroy mutations or raw SQL with that workspace's schema (`getWorkspaceSchemaName`).\n- App-object GraphQL CRUD is reached through `/graphql` (`makeGraphqlAPIRequest`), as demonstrated by `search-caller-permissions.integration-spec.ts` creating `rocket`/`pet` and `composite-unique-index-upsert.integration-spec.ts` creating `compositeUpsertRecord`.\n- Repository-based creation is possible for core entities via `get-core-repository.util.ts` (`global.app.get(getRepositoryToken(...))`), and `testDataSource` is raw SQL. But chat objects are workspace-schema records behind `WorkspaceOrmManager` and permissions; the supported path in tests is the generated GraphQL create mutations, not a core TypeORM repository.\n- Workspace scoping: installing into Apple + forging a foreign token (search spec pattern) gives the cleanest cross-workspace isolation assertion; alternatively create a second workspace (enqueue-job pattern) and install/seed there.\n- Cleanup/teardown: use `cleanupApplicationAndAppRegistration` so a crashed run doesn't leave the registration claimed (the composite-upsert and bilan specs both do this because `syncApplication` fails with `UNIVERSAL_IDENTIFIER_ALREADY_CLAIMED` otherwise).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Suggested placement and shape for the new spec\n\n- New file: `packages/twenty-server/test/integration/graphql/suites/chat/chat-messages.integration-spec.ts` (or `metadata/suites/chat/...` depending on where the resolver is confirmed to be exposed — see §5.4).\n- Cover: (a) newest-first ordering and `hasNextPage`/`endCursor` chaining across pages, (b) `channelId` isolation (messages from another channel absent), (c) `limit` bounds and malformed/foreign cursor behavior, (d) cross-workspace isolation using a forged foreign token, (e) `sendChatTypingIndicator` auth/Forbidden path.\n- Unread counts: no GraphQL surface exists; either add coverage via the realtime read event (`ChatRealtimePublisherService` + `/realtime`, using the realtime jest config) or keep it at the existing unit level.\n\nI did not modify any files.\n</task_result>\n</task>"
